Attribute Stacking — `#test` Combined With Other Attributes
============================================================

Per reference §"Attributes":

> Multiple attributes stack on separate preceding lines. Unknown
> attribute names are stored as-is (no error), so new attributes can
> be introduced incrementally.

This file pins the *attribute grammar* around `#test`:

- Multiple attributes can stack on a single function definition.
- Order does not affect dispatch — `#test #pure` and `#pure #test`
  both yield a registered test.
- An unknown attribute name (e.g., `#custom_marker`) is accepted
  silently — the compiler stores it without erroring, so new
  attributes can be introduced without breaking older code.
- `#deprecated("msg")` with an argument stacks with `#test`.
- The named-test form `#test("name")` stacks with other attributes.
- `#test(should_panic)` can also stack with other attributes.
- Three or more stacked attributes work the same as two.

Note: `#pure` is **enforced** when present. A `#pure` function cannot
call impure functions like `panic`. So a `#pure` + `#test(should_panic)`
combination would *fail to compile* with "cannot call impure function
'panic'". This is correct behaviour; the relevant attribute combination
that stacks cleanly with should_panic is `should_panic + (any unknown
or non-pure attribute)`.

File-local prefix `Tac` (Test-Attr-Combinations).

    module sysl.tests.lang_features


### `#test` + `#pure` — pure tests register and run

A test annotated `#pure` is constrained: it can only call other pure
functions (and built-ins that the analyzer considers pure, like
`assert`). The combination of `#test` + `#pure` registers a normal
test that happens to satisfy the purity check.

    #test
    #pure
    tac_test_pure() -> unit
        assert(2 + 2 == 4, "pure math")

### `#pure` + `#test` — reverse order

Stacking order doesn't matter — `#pure` above `#test` is equivalent.

    #pure
    #test
    tac_pure_test() -> unit
        assert(3 + 3 == 6, "pure-first order")

### `#test` + `#deprecated("...")` — deprecated attribute with arg

`#deprecated("msg")` attaches a deprecation marker. It does not
prevent the function from running — call-site diagnostics are the
deprecation mechanism. Stacked with `#test`, the test still runs.

    #test
    #deprecated("scheduled for removal in v0.5")
    tac_test_deprecated() -> unit
        assert(10 - 3 == 7, "deprecated but still runs")

### `#test` + unknown attribute — unknown is stored, not rejected

Per reference: unknown attribute names are accepted silently. This
lets new attributes be added incrementally.

    #test
    #custom_marker
    tac_test_unknown() -> unit
        assert(8 * 8 == 64, "unknown attr is silent")

### `#test` + unknown attribute with args

Unknown attribute names accept argument lists too — the args are
stored verbatim, no validation.

    #test
    #custom_with_args("foo", 42, true)
    tac_test_unknown_args() -> unit
        assert(5 + 5 == 10, "unknown attr with args silent")

### Three stacked attributes

Two known + one unknown — order chosen to put `#test` second to
verify stacking isn't position-sensitive.

    #deprecated("legacy")
    #test
    #custom_third
    tac_three_attrs() -> unit
        assert(7 + 8 == 15, "three attrs")

### Named `#test("...")` stacks with other attributes

The named-test form composes the same way as the bare `#test`.

    #test("tac — named test stacked with pure")
    #pure
    tac_named_pure() -> unit
        assert(11 * 11 == 121, "named + pure")

### `#test(should_panic)` stacks with an unknown attribute

`should_panic` is a `#test` argument, not a separate attribute.
The combination `#test(should_panic)` + `#custom_marker` puts
should_panic semantics on a test that also carries a custom marker.

    #test(should_panic)
    #custom_marker
    tac_sp_unknown() -> unit
        panic("should-panic-with-marker")

### `#test(should_panic: "...")` stacks with an unknown attribute

Substring-match should_panic + unknown marker.

    #test(should_panic: "marker")
    #custom_label("orange")
    tac_spm_unknown() -> unit
        panic("contains marker substring")
